          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8 | Part-II"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.088,
            0.643,
            0.11
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ What is the area of each triangle in this rectangle?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.113,
            0.693,
            0.168
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We have seen that the diagonal of a rectangle divides it into two congruent triangles. So, the area of each triangle is half the area of the rectangle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.168,
            0.692,
            0.206
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In terms of unit squares, half the area fills exactly half the number of unit squares."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.702,
            0.085,
            0.844,
            0.227
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.702,
                0.085,
                0.844,
                0.227
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_2_image_4.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.214,
            0.613,
            0.238
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So the area of each triangle is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac{1}{2} \\times 7 \\times 4 = 14  cm ^2."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.251,
            0.679,
            0.272
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Why Can’t Perimeter be a Measure of Area?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.283,
            0.846,
            0.341
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Why do we count the number of unit squares to assign measures for area? Couldn’t we have just used the perimeter of a region, i.e., the length of its boundary as a measure of its area?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.355,
            0.846,
            0.414
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "? If two regions have the same perimeter, can’t we conclude that they have the same area? Or, if one region has a larger perimeter than another region, can’t we conclude that it also has a larger area?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.422,
            0.846,
            0.479
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The perimeter of a region is not indicative of its area. The reason is that regions can have the same perimeter but different areas, and vice versa. We can even find two regions, Region 1 and Region 2, such that"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.255,
            0.484,
            0.745,
            0.529
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Perimeter of Region 1 > Perimeter of Region 2, but Area of Region 1 < Area of Region 2."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.537,
            0.845,
            0.578
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Find two rectangles that are examples of such regions. If needed, use a grid paper (given at the end of the book) for this."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.584,
            0.8,
            0.643
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Also give an example of two regions of other shapes, where the region with the larger perimeter has the smaller area! This property should be visually clear in your example."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.815,
            0.584,
            0.902,
            0.642
          ],
          "content": [
